---
// La pagina che Apple pretende per pubblicare un'app. Il testo viene dai
// documenti veri dei tre progetti, non da un modello generico: e' per questo
// che dice cose diverse per ciascuno dei tre motori di Minta invece di una
// riga sola buona per tutto.
//
// La parte di Minta segue l'informativa approvata dal proprietario, versione
// inglese: MintaApp/Documentation/INFORMATIVA-PRIVACY-BOZZA.md (dal 18 ottobre
// 2026 la versione breve, Lotto 26 dell'app). Se cambia quella, cambia questa
// (e i testi nell'app: foglio del consenso e Impostazioni › Info › Privacy).
// Ogni motore e' un blocco .motore a se': si toglie intero, senza toccare il
// resto. Titolare e contatto stanno in cima alla pagina, minori e modifiche in
// fondo: valgono per tutte e tre le app.
//
// La versione italiana e' privacy/it.astro (decisione del proprietario del
// 19 ottobre 2026: il titolare e' in Italia). Le due pagine dicono le stesse
// cose: se ne cambi una, cambia anche l'altra. Questa resta all'indirizzo
// privacy/ perche' l'app e App Store Connect puntano qui.
//
// Le note interne, le verifiche e il ragionamento sul ruolo di titolare
// restano in PRIVACY-BOZZA.md e non si pubblicano.
//
// ATTENZIONE a «Groq keeps nothing»: e' vera (Zero Data Retention) finche'
// quell'interruttore resta acceso nella console di Groq, e nessun test di
// questo sito puo' accorgersi se viene spento. Se tocchi le impostazioni di
// Groq, rileggi questa pagina.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { path } from '../lib/paths';
import '../styles/informativa.css';

const aggiornata = '21 October 2026';
---

<Base
  titolo="Privacy — TROVA.ME"
  descrizione="What each TROVA.ME app does with your data, in plain terms. Minta keeps your recordings on your iPhone and sends them out only after you say yes."
>
  <div class="contenitore informativa">
    <nav class="lingue" aria-label="Language">
      <span aria-current="page">English</span>
      <span aria-hidden="true">·</span>
      <a href={path('privacy/it/')} lang="it" hreflang="it">Italiano</a>
    </nav>

    <h1>Privacy</h1>

    <p class="apertura">
      TROVA.ME is one person making three iPhone apps. This page says what each app does with
      your data, in plain terms. It is short because the apps collect very little.
    </p>

    <p>
      The data controller is <strong>Daniele Riccarand</strong>, acting as an individual, in
      Milan, Italy. For anything on this page, write to <a href="mailto:dan@trova.me"
        >dan@trova.me</a
      >.
    </p>

    <h2>This website</h2>
    <p>
      This site has no analytics, no cookies, no trackers and no contact form. Nothing you do
      here is recorded. It is a set of static pages served by GitHub Pages which — like any
      web server — logs requests at its own level; TROVA.ME neither receives nor stores those
      logs.
    </p>

    <h2>Minta</h2>
    <p>
      Minta records audio, transcribes it, and rewrites the transcript. <strong>In short:
      Minta keeps nothing of your recordings.</strong> They stay on your iPhone (and in your
      iCloud, if you turn on sync). They leave only for a job you ask for, after your yes, and
      Minta Cloud is only a passage. No ads, analytics or tracking.
    </p>

    <h3>On your iPhone</h3>
    <p>
      Recordings, transcriptions, write-ups, notes and your glossary stay on the phone until you
      delete them, and can end up in your device backups. Your Minta Cloud sign-in is in the iOS
      encrypted keychain. These also stay on the phone:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>speaker recognition</strong> (off until you turn it on): it tells the voices
        apart (A, B, C…) with a model on the phone; no voice prints, no recognizing who people
        are. You type the voices’ names and they stay with the recording;
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Spotlight</strong>: the name, date and first sentence of the write-up go into
        the iPhone’s search (it can be turned off in Settings);
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Export</strong>: the .minta file (audio, texts and place) or the audio copy goes
        only where you send it.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3>When data leaves</h3>
    <p>
      Only for a job you ask for, or one that starts by itself if you turn on “Automatic run”.
      The first time for each engine Minta says what is sent and to whom, and asks your
      permission. The format’s instructions, your glossary terms and the voices (letters or
      names) go with the text.
    </p>

    {/* Un motore, un blocco: ognuno si toglie intero senza toccare gli altri. */}
    <div class="motore">
      <h4>On the phone</h4>
      <p>
        The on-device engine offline, and Apple Speech: nothing leaves. If speech recognition on
        the phone isn’t available, Minta asks you every time before using Apple’s servers.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="motore">
      <h4>Apple Intelligence (Private Cloud Compute)</h4>
      <p>
        Online and with your yes, the text goes to Apple’s Private Cloud Compute servers, which
        write the write-up. Apple uses it only to answer, doesn’t keep it, nobody at Apple can
        see it, and independent experts can verify this.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="motore">
      <h4>Minta Cloud</h4>
      <p>
        The text (or, for Whisper, a compressed copy of the audio) goes to Minta’s server in
        Frankfurt, which passes it to the provider doing the work: <strong>Anthropic</strong> (Claude)
        writes the write-ups; <strong>Groq</strong> transcribes with Whisper and, rarely,
        writes a backup write-up when Anthropic is unavailable (never with your own formats).
        They work on Minta’s behalf, in the United States, under the EU Standard Contractual Clauses, and
        don’t use the data to train their models: Groq keeps nothing, Anthropic deletes
        everything within 30 days.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="motore">
      <h4>iCloud sync</h4>
      <p>
        Off until you turn it on. Your recordings go to <strong>your own</strong> iCloud, at
        Apple, not to Minta’s server. The texts are end-to-end encrypted; Apple keeps the audio
        like your other iCloud data. Turning it off deletes nothing: what is in iCloud you delete
        from the iCloud settings.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h3>Minta Cloud account</h3>
    <p>
      You sign in with Sign in with Apple: Minta gets only a code Apple creates, no name and no
      email. Apple handles payments: Minta never sees your payment details.
    </p>

    <h3>What Minta’s server keeps, and for how long</h3>
    <dl class="conservazione">
      <div>
        <dt>Audio and texts</dt>
        <dd>
          Never stored. The result stays in memory for at most 15 minutes, to recover a request
          interrupted by iOS.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Credit, free trial, charges and purchases, tied to your Sign in with Apple code</dt>
        <dd>While you have the account; gone from backups within 24 hours of deleting it.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>
          A random support code, shown in Settings › Minta Cloud: if you include it when you
          write to support, it is used only to find your account
        </dt>
        <dd>While you have the account; gone from backups within 24 hours of deleting it.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Technical logs (durations, lengths, error codes; never texts, audio, names or emails)</dt>
        <dd>30 days.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>
          After you delete the account: accounting records no longer linked to you, with Apple’s
          transaction code for refunds
        </dt>
        <dd>10 years (tax obligations).</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>After you delete the account: a one-way code of your Apple identifier</dt>
        <dd>Only to avoid a second free trial.</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>

    <p>
      <strong>Why we process it.</strong> To give you the service you ask for (contract);
      accounting records because the law requires it; the trial’s anti-abuse code for our
      legitimate interest in preventing abuse.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>iPhone permissions.</strong> Microphone to record, speech recognition to
      transcribe, notifications for alerts, Live Activities to show on the Lock Screen the
      recording in progress or being worked on, with its name (it stays on the phone).
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Your rights.</strong> You can ask to see, correct or delete your data, or object
      to its use, by writing to <a href="mailto:dan@trova.me">dan@trova.me</a>; you delete your
      account yourself in the app (Settings › Minta Cloud; any remaining credit is lost). You
      can also contact your data protection authority (in Italy, the Garante privacy, <a
        href="https://www.garanteprivacy.it">garanteprivacy.it</a
      >).
    </p>

    <h2>Pronto</h2>
    <p>
      Your list lives on your iPhone. <strong>There is no Pronto account and no Pronto
      server.</strong>
    </p>
    <p>
      To understand what you dictate, Pronto sends what you said — together with the tasks
      already on your list, so that “move the dentist to Thursday” has something to refer to
      — to the AI provider you chose. That exchange happens directly between your iPhone and
      the provider, with your own key, under your own account and their terms. TROVA.ME is
      not part of it and never sees the key, which is stored in the iPhone’s keychain. If you
      never configure a provider, nothing is sent anywhere.
    </p>

    <h2>ClaudePal</h2>
    <p>
      ClaudePal is an experiment and is not distributed. It connects your iPhone to a Claude
      Code session running on your own Mac, over your own local network. Nothing is sent to
      TROVA.ME. What Claude Code itself sends to Anthropic is governed by Anthropic’s terms
      and by your own account.
    </p>
    <p>
      The connection is not encrypted and is intended for a trusted network only. This is
      stated plainly on the ClaudePal page as well.
    </p>

    <h2>Children</h2>
    <p>None of these apps are directed at children.</p>

    <h2>Changes</h2>
    <p>
      If any of this changes, this page changes with it, and the date below changes too.
    </p>

    <p class="stato">Last updated: {aggiornata}</p>
  </div>
</Base>
